[[impact-analysis]]
Impact analysis and blast radius

Impact analysis answers the question every refactor begins with: if I change this,
what else breaks? Starting from a single symbol, the analysis walks the graph in the
downstream direction and collects everything transitively reachable — the functions
that call it, the types that embed it, the modules that depend on those, and so on
outward until the reachable set stops growing. That set is the change's blast radius.

A blast radius is more useful than a flat list of direct callers because breakage
propagates. A type whose field changes can ripple through several layers before it
surfaces as a failure, and only a transitive walk catches the far edge of that
ripple. Pairing the walk with a git diff lets the tool report the blast radius of a
specific pending change and attach a coarse risk level to it before you commit.
